A couple of quibbles:  first, the main effect of a vitamin "overdose" in my
experience and observation, is sleepiness (and a characteristic deep
sleep).  (Before anyone tells me, I know that an overdose of vitamin A or D
or iron (or selenium) can kill.)

Another point I'd like to make is that vitamins weren't discovered until
about the 1920s.  NetHack is overall a game set in a medieval universe, so
it always bothers me to find anything made of, say, aluminum or, e.g. "an
iridium wand".  Couldn't such items be replaced with something more
appropriate, i.e. made of a material known at the time and also believed to
possess magical properties?

(If you want a use for vitamins that may have some meaning in game terms,
I've found high doses of vitamin C to greatly increase my tolerance of cold.)

Seriously, is there any use for vitamins in the game that couldn't be
handled through potions?  Fortified woozleberry juice, anyone?

Do you really want vitamins in NetHack?  What about

	- steroids:  increase strength for a limited time, may cause
	permanent damage or death, may cause you to go berserk, has
	androgenic properties for female characters

	- pennicillin:  cure disease (not wounds themselves, but may keep
	them from getting infected); won't necessarily cure everything

	- uppers:  allows you to do more damager per hit, keep fighting when
	starved, move or pick up boulders you otherwise couldn't.  results
	in an eventual crash, with lowered con and possibly a need for a big
	sleep and either more food or more uppers.  these could also cause
	paranoia:  like hallucination, but you think every monster is more
	powerful than it is, nothing appears to be tame, and you think
	shopkeepers are hitting you when they really aren't.

Before actually proposing to add any such suggested features, I would
recommend asking:

Will any of this really help you gain the fabled Amulet of Yendor?  Most
importantly, will it increase your enjoyment of the game?


